                                                                          "next": {
                                                                            "block": {
                                                                              "type": "abs_se_senao",
                                                                              "id": "1o=XqsA;RSVS(vQluG(y",
                                                                              "icons": {
                                                                                "comment": {
                                                                                  "text": "What decides here is the REAL Certainty Degree, not the Certainty\nDegree. The difference is not subtle. The Para-Analyzer tests the\nCertainty Degree before the Contradiction Degree, so whatever passes\nthe V test never reaches the T test. With evid1 = 1.00 and evid2 =\n0.60 we get Gc = +0.6 and Gct = +0.4: the two sensors disagree by more\nthan half the part tolerance, and the algorithm answers V. The part\npasses. The Real Certainty Degree is, by definition, the certainty\nfree from the effect of contradiction: Gcr = 1 - D, with D = sqrt((1 -\n|Gc|)^2 + Gct^2). In this same case it is +0.434, stays below the\ncutoff, and the part goes back to be measured again. As a bonus comes\nthe NAP indefiniteness guard (phi <= 0.25 or D > 1), which the real\ncertainty block already applies inside: too much contradiction returns\nzero, and zero neither approves nor rejects. A single quantity,\ninstead of an invented guard",
                                                                                  "pinned": false,
                                                                                  "height": 80,
                                                                                  "width": 160
                                                                                }
